    "q-30282": {
      "dom_id": "step-q30282",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/9a448574651a74ef7180549827e05ee2d790cd9fa89c6c85adf08d8444ceaadf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({10}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({10}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({10}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({10}^{x})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k({10}^{0})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 1 & =k-3 \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4({10}^{x})-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
