    "q-18519": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18519",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/9507e060ddd7de93489b236b48856870e2c884eba69f63ac67662dfd1c061c17.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ so $c=1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k({2}^{0})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k(1)+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.5({2}^{x})+1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
